{:kotoba.wasi/format :v1
 :version "0.3.0"
 :source {:repository "https://github.com/WebAssembly/WASI"
          :revision "3ee2a590c766594ae44a54730fc74fc27da5c609"
          :path "proposals/<proposal>/wit"
          :promoted "2026-06-11"}
 :vendored
 {:clocks ["types.wit" "monotonic-clock.wit" "system-clock.wit"
           "timezone.wit" "world.wit"]
  ;; http is vendored ahead of any provider that uses it, so
  ;; `kotoba.component.wasi-contract` can resolve the `:provider-wasi`
  ;; declarations that name it. Its own `deps` (cli, random, clocks) are NOT
  ;; vendored: the contract check reads interface names out of these files
  ;; and does not run a WIT resolve. Building an http provider will need
  ;; them.
  :http ["types.wit" "worlds.wit"]}
 :note
 "Vendored verbatim so provider components build offline against the exact
  revision `component-model-v1.edn` pins. Do not edit these files: a local
  edit would make the composed world claim an interface the engine does not
  implement, and the failure would surface as a linker error at run time
  rather than as a contract diff.

  Two 0.2.x names sat in `component-model-v1.edn` until 2026-08-11, both
  naming interfaces 0.3.0 does not define:

  - `wasi:clocks/wall-clock` -> renamed `system-clock`, and its result
    became `instant { seconds: s64, nanoseconds: u32 }`.
  - `wasi:http/outgoing-handler` -> replaced by `client` (`send`) and
    `handler` (`handle`), both `async func`.

  `kotoba.component.wasi-contract` now resolves every declared import
  against these files, so a third one cannot be written unnoticed."}
